  "constraints": "* Input arrays/tensors are non-empty and well-formed\n* Floating point values are finite real numbers\n* Numerical operations must adhere to standard precision tolerances",
  "starterCode": "import numpy as np\n\ndef numpy_boolean_masking(matrix: list, threshold: float = 5.0) -> dict:\n    pass",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **Boolean Masking** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef numpy_boolean_masking(matrix: list, threshold: float = 5.0) -> dict:\n    arr = np.array(matrix, dtype=np.float64)\n    th = float(threshold)\n    return {'element_mask': (arr > th).tolist(), 'filtered_rows': arr[np.any(arr > th, axis=1)].tolist(), 'extracted_elements': arr[arr > th].tolist()}\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
